"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[7811],{7811:function(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"testId":"ophthalmology-test-2","slug":"ophthalmology","subject":"Ophthalmology","testNo":2,"durationMin":40,"questions":[{"n":1,"subject":"Ophthalmology","text":"A middle-imaged female patient presents to the clinic with sudden onset mild ptosis, decreased sweating on the same side, and anisocoria. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":["Adie\'s tonic pupil","Horner syndrome","Bell\'s palsy","Argyll Robertson pupil"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Horner syndrome\\n\\nThe likely diagnosis in a patient with sudden onset mild ptosis, decreased sweating on the same side and anisocoria is Horner syndrome.\\n\\nHorner syndrome\xa0is also termed oculosympathetic palsy.\xa0Presentation depends on the level of lesion in the sympathetic chain.\\n\\nPatients commonly present with symptoms such as\\n\\n• Mild ptosis\\n• Anisocoria (difference in size of the pupils) accentuated in dim light\\n• Reduced ipsilateral sweating\\n\\nTests with topical apraclonidine and cocaine can be used to confirm a diagnosis of Horner syndrome.\\n\\nOther options\\n\\nOption A: Adie\'s tonic pupil is caused by damaged sphincter pupillae and ciliary muscle following a viral illness. Patients present with large regular pupils that have absent/sluggish light reflexes.\\n\\nOption C: Bell\'s palsy is an idiopathic facial palsy affecting lower motor neurons. There is an ipsilateral restriction of eye closure and facial muscle weakness.\\n\\nOption D: Argyll Robertson pupil is caused by neurosyphilis. On examination, there are irregular small pupils that do not change in size in bright light. However, they constrict on accommodation.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":2,"subject":"Ophthalmology","text":"A neonate is brought with complaints of photophobia and watering. Clinical examination shows normal tear passages and a clear, but large, cornea as well as Haab’s striae. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":["Buphthalmos","Trachoma","Keratoglobus","Keratoconus"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Buphthalmos\\n\\nThe given clinical scenario is suggestive of\xa0buphthalmos.\xa0Haab’s striae are horizontal/circumferential breaks in Descemet’s membrane seen in\xa0buphthalmos.\\n\\nBuphthalmos (bull-like eyes) is an enlargement of the eyeball seen in true congenital and infantile glaucoma, usually when the disease onset is before 3 years.\\n\\nThe image given below shows\xa0Haab\'s striae and a child having buphthalmos in congenital glaucoma.\\n\\nNote:\\n\\nVogt\'s striae\xa0are vertical,\xa0fine, whitish lines (folds/tears) in Descemet\'s membrane commonly found in patients with keratoconus.\\n\\nThe image given below shows Vogt\'s striae.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/be20cc0a661b099757f6.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/2ebbe0106b82585b169f.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/4e002d6fa0355d3247f8.jpg"}]},{"n":3,"subject":"Ophthalmology","text":"A post-cataract surgery patient who had the surgery 3 months back presents with complaints of blurring of vision for the past 2 weeks. On fundoscopy, there is a honeycomb appearance. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":["Cystoid macular edema","Macular hole","Central serous retinopathy","After cataract"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Cystoid macular edema\\n\\nThe clinical scenario of a\xa0post-cataract surgery patient presenting with a blurring of vision and a honeycomb appearance on fundoscopy\xa0is suggestive of\xa0cystoid macular edema (CME).\\n\\nCommon causes of CME include previous cataract surgery, uveitis, central retinal vein occlusions, diabetic retinopathy, retinitis pigmentosa, retinopathy of prematurity, and topical prostaglandins. Cystoid macular edema in post-cataract surgery patients is termed as Irvine-Gass syndrome.\\n\\nIn CME, optical coherence tomography (OCT) shows loss of foveal depression, intraretinal cystoid spaces, and retinal thickening. CME occurs due to a break in the inner and outer blood-retinal barrier. Due to retinal vessel leak fluid or exudate comes out and forms cystic space in the central retina. Mostly it gets accumulated in the outer plexiform (Henle\'s layer) and inner nuclear layers of the retina.\\n\\nOn fundoscopy, the multiple cystoid oval spaces in CME appear as a\xa0 honeycomb appearance. In Fundus fluorescein angiography fluid accumulation in the outer plexiform, layer shows a characteristic flower petal pattern (petaloid appearance) as shown below:\\n\\nTopical antiprostaglandins and steroids are used in the management of CME.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B- Macular hole is the partial or full-thickness hole in the neurosensory retina. An OCT image of a full-thickness macular hole is shown below:\\n\\nOption C- Central serous retinopathy is the spontaneous serous detachment of the neuroretina in the macular region with or without retinal pigment epithelium detachment.\\n\\nDue to the altered permeability of the choroidal vascular system, there is an increase in the tissue hydrostatic pressure in the choroid. This causes a breach in the outer blood-retinal barrier. Leakage of fluid across this area causes detachment of the neurosensory retina.\xa0An OCT of central serous retinopathy showing neurosensory retinal detachment from the retinal pigment epithelium is given below:\\n\\nOption D- Posterior capsular opacification, otherwise known as\xa0after cataract/secondary cataract,\xa0is the\xa0most common late complication\xa0of uncomplicated cataract surgery. It occurs due to the\xa0proliferation\xa0of\xa0lens\xa0epithelial cells\xa0that have been left out inside the capsular bag after cataract surgery.\\n\\nThey can present in different patterns such as:\\n\\n• Elschnig\'s pearls: present as\xa0vacuolated (pearl-type) grape-like clusters of swollen cells\\n• Soemmering\'s ring: present as a white annular proliferation that is formed at the periphery of the capsular bag.\\n• Fibrosis type: present as fibroblastic metaplasia of the epithelial cells\\n\\nA posterior capsulotomy\xa0(creating a hole in the posterior capsule) using an\xa0Nd: YAG laser is the mainstay of treatment. It is indicated when the patient is symptomatic.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/a6fd4304b39baf4b7b7e.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/ee1d228688249b0cbcb5.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/8f383ea41b75821bc85b.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/01427058afd6f954a454.jpg"}]},{"n":4,"subject":"Ophthalmology","text":"The visual field defect usually found in craniopharyngioma is _____.","options":["Bitemporal hemianopia","Binasal hemianopia","Superior quadrantanopia","Inferior quadrantanopia"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Bitemporal hemianopia\\n\\nThe visual field defect usually found in craniopharyngioma is bitemporal hemianopia.\\n\\nCraniopharyngiomas are\xa0benign masses\xa0usually presenting in the\xa0suprasellar region\xa0arising near the pituitary stalk and can produce\xa0functional impairment\xa0of the hypophysis due to\xa0mass effect. The clinical features of craniopharyngiomas include headaches, visual field defects, growth failure and delayed sexual maturation due to pituitary hypofunction. CT scan may show calcifications in the supra-sellar region. Treatment includes trans-sphenoidal resection of the tumor followed by adjuvant chemotherapy.\\n\\nLesions of the optic chiasma can cause bitemporal hemianopia,\xa0i.e, loss of vision in the temporal halves of both visual fields. Nasal fibers of each retina cross through the chiasma at this level, and hence, these fibers are compromised.\\n\\nOther causes of chiasmal lesions are\\n\\n• Pituitary adenoma (most common)\\n• Aneurysm of the anterior communicating artery\\n• Meningioma\\n• Glioma\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B) Binasal hemianopia is a rare visual field defect also caused due to lesions at the optic chiasma.\\n\\nOption C) Superior quadrantanopia is also known as pie-in-the-sky defect and is caused when lesions affect the inferior fibers of the optic radiations in the temporal lobe.\\n\\nOption D)\xa0Inferior quadrantanopia\xa0is also known as pie-on-the-floor defect and is caused due to lesions affecting the superior fibers of the optic radiations in the parietal lobe.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":5,"subject":"Ophthalmology","text":"During ward rounds in Ophthalmology, there was a discussion about various modes of ultrasound scan. Which of the following is not an indication for a B mode scan?","options":["Evaluation of cause of papilledema","Study of intraocular tumours","Measurement of anterior chamber depth","Assessment of the vitreous"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Measurement of anterior chamber depth\\n\\nAssessment of anterior chamber depth\xa0is done by \\"A\\" scan. The rest are all indications for a B scan.\\n\\nBoth \'A\' and \'B\' scans are types of ultrasound (USG) scans.\\n\\nA scan (time amplitude)\\nB scan (intensity modulation)\\n\\nIt\xa0produces a unidimensional image and echoes are plotted as spikes.\\nIt\xa0produces a\xa0two-dimensional dotted section of the eyeball.\\n\\nUses:\\n\\n• Axial length\\n• Intraocular lens (IOL) power calculation: Biometry\\n• Anterior chamber depth\\n• The thickness of intraocular mass\\n\\nUses:\\n\\n• Assessment of the posterior segment in opaque media\\n• Study of intraocular tumors\\n• Localization of intraocular and intra-orbital foreign bodies\\n\\nThe first image below shows \\"A\\" scan ultrasound amplitude spikes.\\n\\nThe second image shows a normal \\"B\\" scan of the eye.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/1c47c10bd1614156871b.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/9761a9bec961bca0c978.jpg"}]},{"n":6,"subject":"Ophthalmology","text":"Which of the following test is not used to assess color vision?","options":["Farnsworth–Munsell 100-hue test","Holmgren\'s wools test","Amsler\'s grid test","Ishihara’s chart"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Amsler\'s grid test\\n\\nAmsler\'s grid test\xa0is not used to assess color vision. It is used to assess macular pathology.\\n\\nCommonly employed color vision tests include:\\n\\n• Ishihara’s chart\\n• Farnsworth–Munsell 100-hue test\\n• Hardy–Rand–Rittler test\\n• Edridge-Green lantern test\\n• City University test\\n• Holmgrens wool-matching test\\n• Naegels anamoloscope\\n\\nThe below image shows the Farnsworth–Munsell 100-hue test,\xa0which is used\xa0to test color vision.\xa0Farnsworth D15 hue discrimination test is similar to the below\xa0test but it utilizes only 15 hue caps.\\n\\nIshihara\'s chart\xa0(image below):\\n\\nHolmgrens wool test\xa0(image below):\\n\\nAmsler grid test\xa0is used in\xa0detecting maculopathy.\\n\\nOther uses include:\\n\\n• Assess macular function\\n• Evaluate the visual field of 10\xb0\\n• Good for follow-up in CSR (Central serous retinopathy)\\n\\nThe image below shows a normal Amsler\'s grid.\\n\\nMetamorphopsia is a condition in which patients\xa0perceive distorted images\xa0of the object, which occurs in macular pathology.\xa0The image below shows\xa0metamorphopsia\xa0seen on\xa0Amsler\'s grid.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/a37e5c343979bbd53614.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/028f33388239ee6dba25.png"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/ba72edf3af08bcb9ef19.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/92fac623809312476fb2.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/1cf2c7e7985182fd62f2.jpg"}]},{"n":7,"subject":"Ophthalmology","text":"Which of the following is not a disease included under Global Vision 2020 Program, but included recently in Vision 2020 program for India?","options":["Childhood blindness","Cataract","Refractive error","Glaucoma"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Glaucoma\\n\\nGlaucoma\xa0is not included in the Global Vision 2020 Program but is included under\xa0Vision 2020 program for India.\\n\\nDiseases targeted under Vision 2020\\n\\nS. No\\nGlobally\\nIndia\\n\\n1.\\n\\nCataract\\n\\nCataract\\n\\n2.\\n\\nRefractive errors\\n\\nRefractive errors\\n\\n3.\\n\\nChildhood blindness\\n\\nChildhood blindness\\n\\n4.\\n\\nOnchocerciasis\\n\\nGlaucoma\\n\\n5.\\n\\nTrachoma\\n\\nDiabetic retinopathy\\n\\n6.\\n\\nLow vision\\n\\nCorneal blindness","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":8,"subject":"Ophthalmology","text":"Internal hordeolum is due to inflammation of:","options":["Meibomian gland","Mohl gland","Lacrimal gland","Zeis gland"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Meibomian gland\\n\\nInternal hordeolum is due to inflammation of\xa0Meibomian gland.\\n\\nHordeolum internum is the suppurative inflammation of the Meibomian gland and may occur as a primary infection or secondary to infected chalazion. There is a tender swelling with the pus point facing toward the tarsal conjunctiva. The below image shows an internal hordeolum.\\n\\nA chalazion is a chronic and\xa0non-infective\xa0lipogranulamatous lesion\xa0that arises from the\xa0Meibomian glands (otherwise known as tarsal/meibomian cyst). It is the most common lid swelling seen. A chalazion may become infected and form a\xa0hordeolum internum. Also, a\xa0recurrently forming chalazion should be biopsied to rule out\xa0meibomian gland adenocarcinoma (a form of sebaceous cell carcinoma).\\n\\nHordeolum externum\xa0or\xa0stye is the suppurative inflammation of the gland of Zeis. It is hard and painful, and there is a visible pus point on the lid margin.\\n\\nBelow attached is an image of a patient with hordeolum externum:","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/fd8f27055e248d014370.png"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/24585138c5116462e60d.jpg"}]},{"n":9,"subject":"Ophthalmology","text":"Which is the most common cause of staphyloma at point D in the given image?","options":["Glaucoma","Trauma","Degenerative high axial myopia","Scleritis"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Degenerative high axial myopia\\n\\nDegenerative high axial myopia is the most common cause for posterior staphyloma (Point D)\\n\\nStaphyloma:\\n\\n• It is an abnormal protrusion of the\xa0uveal tissue through a weak and thin portion of the\xa0cornea\xa0or sclera.\\n• So, staphyloma is lined internally by uveal tissue and externally by weak cornea or sclera.\\n• Types of Staphyloma:\\n\\n• Anterior\xa0staphyloma: It is characterized\xa0by ectasia of the pseudo-cornea, which occurs after the total sloughing of the\xa0cornea. It is lined internally by the iris and externally by the newly formed epithelium.\\n• Intercalary staphyloma: This is located at the limbus and is lined by the root of the iris and the anterior-most part of the ciliary body\\n• Ciliary staphyloma: It is characterized by ectasia of weak sclera lined by the\xa0ciliary body. This affects the ciliary zone, which includes the region up to 8 mm behind the limbus.\\n• Equatorial staphyloma: This occurs at the equatorial region up to 14 mm behind the limbus of the eye.\\n• Posterior staphyloma: This affects the posterior pole of the eye.\\n\\n• Note :\\n\\n• The most common cause for anterior staphyloma is sloughing corneal ulcer.\\n• The most common cause for posterior staphyloma is degenerative high axial myopia.\\n\\n• In the image:\\n\\n• A: Intercalary staphyloma\\n• B: Ciliary staphyloma\\n• C: Equatorial staphyloma\\n• D: Posterior staphyloma","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/9d40be703fb3497715c6.jpg"]},{"n":10,"subject":"Ophthalmology","text":"Which is the most common epithelial lacrimal gland tumor?","options":["Malignant mixed tumor","Pleomorphic adenoma","Mucoepidermoid carcinoma","Adenoid cystic carcinoma"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Pleomorphic adenoma\\n\\nPleomorphic adenoma is the most common epithelial lacrimal gland tumor.\\n\\nThe most common epithelial tumors, in order of frequency, are as follows:\\n\\n• Benign mixed tumor (BMT), also known as\xa0pleomorphic adenoma (PA)\\n• Adenoid cystic carcinoma (ACC)\\n• Pleomorphic adenocarcinoma (ACA)\\n• Mucoepidermoid carcinoma\\n\\nMost Commons:\\n\\nMost common tumor of lacrimal gland: Pleomorphic adenoma\\n\\nMost common epithelial tumor of lacrimal gland: Pleomorphic adenoma\\n\\nMost common malignant epithelial tumor of lacrimal gland: Adenoid cystic carcinoma","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":11,"subject":"Ophthalmology","text":"A preterm baby in the neonatal intensive care unit is being screened for retinopathy of prematurity (ROP). She is found to have stage 2 ROP in zone I with no plus disease. What is the recommended treatment for this stage of the disease?","options":["Laser ablation","Intravitreal bevacizumab injection","Pars plana vitrectomy","Careful observation"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Careful observation\\n\\nThe recommended treatment for ROP stage 2 in zone I (categorized as type I/low-risk ROP) is careful observation and follow-up.\\n\\nRetinopathy of prematurity occurs in premature infants with low birth weight, with a typical history of exposure to oxygen in high concentrations leading to bilateral proliferative retinopathy.\\n\\nStages of Retinopathy of Prematurity:\\n\\n• Stage 1- demarcation line formation at the edge of vessels, dividing the vascular from the avascular retina.\\n• Stage 2- intraretinal ridge formation\\n• Stage 3- ridge with extraretinal fibrovascular proliferation into the vitreous.\\n• Stage 4-\xa0subtotal retinal detachment\\n• Stage 5- total retinal detachment\\n• \'Plus\' disease is characterized by dilatation and tortuosity of blood vessels in at least two quadrants of the posterior fundus. It signifies the progression of the disease.\\n\\nLocation:\\n\\nZone I: Circular area with a radius twice the distance from disc to the fovea, with the disc as the center. (as shown in the image)\\nZone II: It covers the area beyond zone I circumferentially till nasal ora serrata.\\nZone III: It consists of residual temporal crescent anterior to zone II.\\n\\nThese zones are shown in the image below:\\n\\nTreatment is based on guidelines set by the early treatment of retinopathy of prematurity trial (ETROP).\\n\\nType 1: Treatment within 72 hours (High risk)\\n\\n• Any ROP + plus disease\\n• Stage 3 ROP in zone I\\n• Stage 2/3 in zone II + plus disease.\\n\\nType 2: Observation (Low risk)\\n\\n• Stage 1/2 in zone I only and no plus disease\\n• Stage 3 ROP within zone II and no plus disease.\\n\\nThis can be summarized in the table below:\\n\\nStage 1\\nStage 2\\nStage 3\\n\\nZone I\\nObservation\\nObservation\\nTreatment\\n\\nZone II - without plus disease\\n\\nObservation\\nObservation\\n\\nZone II- with plus disease\\n\\nTreatment\\nTreatment\\n\\nROP of any stage in any zone,\xa0if associated with \'plus\' disease,\xa0should\xa0receive treatment urgently.\\n\\nTreatment includes laser ablation of the avascular peripheral retina, anti-VEGF medication (bevacizumab), and pars plana vitrectomy for advanced disease. The high-risk patients requiring treatment should receive it within 72 hours.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/77046a0d6b7a3c15d102.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/0d2f5da644a2947c717e.gif"}]},{"n":12,"subject":"Ophthalmology","text":"A 61-year-old university professor presents with a loss of vision in his left eye. He describes the sensation as a curtain being drawn over his affected eye. In addition, 7 days ago the patient noticed what he described as flashing lights and floaters. He gives a history of having cataract surgery in the same eye 3 weeks ago. A B-scan image of his left eye is shown below. What could be his condition?","options":["Retinal detachment","Corneal ulcer","Glaucoma","Retinal tumour"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Retinal detachment\\n\\nThe given clinical scenario and the B-scan suggest a diagnosis of rhegmatogenous retinal detachment. It\xa0is due to the accumulation of fluid between neurosensory retina(NSR) and retinal pigment epithelium (RPE)\xa0due to a retinal tear.\\n\\nDefinition of retinal detachment: The separation of the neurosensory retina from the RPE.\\n\\nTypes of Retinal detachment:\\n\\nA. Rhegmatogenous retinal detachment- fluid accumulates after a break (tear) occurs in the retina.\\n\\n• Causes:\\n\\n• High myopes\\n• Retinoschisis-splitting of neurosensory retina plus vitreous degeneration\\n\\n• Typical\xa0retinoschisis-split at the\xa0level of the\xa0outer plexiform layer\\n• Atypical\xa0retinoschisis-split at the\xa0level of the\xa0nerve fiber layer\\n\\n• Trauma\\n• Pseudophakia\\n• Aphakia\\n\\n• The most dangerous type of retinal break is horseshoe tear.\\n• The most dangerous location of retinal break is the\xa0supero-temporal retina.\\n• Clinical features\\n\\n• Photopsia-flashes of light due to vitreoretinal traction at the\xa0site of the break\\n• Floaters\\n• Visual field defect- a\xa0black curtain/veil in front of the eye\\n\\n• Signs\\n\\n• Hypotony-\xa0as the liquefied vitreous in the subretinal space is absorbed through the RPE.\\n• Shafers sign-pigments in the anterior vitreous (tobacco dusting) are a feature of fresh retinal detachment.\\n• The detached retina is convex in configuration.\xa0The surface is corrugated with undulations.\\n\\n• Treatment\\n\\n• Prophylactic laser barrage\\n• Scleral buckling\\n• Pneumatic retinopexy\\n• Pars plana vitrectomy\\n\\nB. Tractional retinal detachment:\xa0Fibro-vascular membranes in the vitreous due to long-standing retinal neovascularization\xa0exert traction on the retina.\\n\\n• Causes\\n\\n• Proliferative diabetic retinopathy\\n• Central retinal venous occlusion\\n• Eales disease\\n• Retinopathy of Prematurity(ROP)\\n\\n• Signs\\n\\n• The detached retina is concave in configuration. No breaks are seen\\n\\n• Treatment\\n\\n• Pars plana vitrectomy with endo photocoagulation.\\n\\nC. Exudative retinal detachment: Exudative fluid from the choroid, collects in the subretinal space leading to retinal detachment.\\n\\n• Causes\\n\\n• Inflammatory conditions like choroiditis, posterior scleritis\\n• Choroidal tumours like melanoma\\n• Toxaemia of pregnancy\\n• Malignant hypertension\\n• Coats disease\\n\\n• Clinical features\\n\\n• Floaters due to vitritis but no photopsia\\n• Visual field defect\\n\\n• Signs\\n\\n• The detached retina is convex in configuration.\xa0The surface\xa0is smooth. No break is seen.\\n• Shifting fluid is seen.\\n\\n• Treatment\\n\\n• Systemic steroids\\n• Treatment of the cause\\n\\nThe image below shows a fundus which reveals retinal detachment inferiorly from 3-9 o\'clock with a tear at 3:30","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/57097d84563d8b97b9bc.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/77f35daf16f8b1a5336a.jpg"}]},{"n":13,"subject":"Ophthalmology","text":"A worried mother brought her son for vision examination when she noticed him reading a book too closely. She says he also narrows his eyes while watching television. Examination revealed myopia. Which of the following lens should be prescribed to correct this child\'s vision?","options":["Cylindrical","Concave + convex","Convex","Concave"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Concave\\n\\nConcave lenses are used for correcting myopic errors.\\n\\nMyopia, also known as \'short sight\', is that dioptric condition of the eye in which, with the\xa0accommodation at rest, incident parallel rays come to a focus anterior to the light-sensitive layer of the retina.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nConvex lenses are used in the correction of hypermetropic eye and presbyopic eye.\\n\\nCylindrical lenses are used in the correction of astigmatism.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/43601d2966eed5a89d9e.jpg"}]},{"n":14,"subject":"Ophthalmology","text":"Internuclear ophthalmoplegia is due to a lesion in the _______.","options":["Pretectal fibres","Occipital lobe","Medial longitudinal fasciculus","Paramedian pontine reticular formation (PPRF)"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Medial longitudinal fasciculus\\n\\nLesion to the medial longitudinal fasciculus causes internulcear ophthalmoplegia.\\n\\nInternuclear ophthalmoplegia\\n\\n• Lesion to medial longitudinal fasciculus (MLF)\\n• Seen in multiple sclerosis\\n\\nAnatomy\\n\\n• After initiation, horizontal eye movements are generated in the\xa0horizontal gaze centre (PPRF) and mediated via a common pathway.\\n• From here, impulses travel\\n\\n• directly to the ipsilateral sixth nerve nucleus and\\n• indirectly via internuclear neurons that cross the midline at the level of the pons and pass up the contralateral medial longitudinal fasciculus (MLF), to motor neurones in the medial rectus subnucleus of the contralateral third nerve complex.\\n\\n• Stimulation of the horizontal gaze center on one side, thereby causing a conjugate movement of the eyes to the same side.\\n\\nFeatures:\\n\\n• Defect in ipsilateral adduction: Medial rectus defect\\n• Horizontal diplopia\\n• Nystagmus of opposite eye\\n• Convergence is normal\\n\\nImage: Internuclear ophthalmoplegia","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/b441da4b71b7cfa74968.jpg"}]},{"n":15,"subject":"Ophthalmology","text":"An 18-year-old boy presents with a history of injury to the eye caused while climbing a tree 5 days ago; the injury has caused pain, photophobia, and redness of the eye for 2 days. He is diagnosed with a fungal ulcer. What of the following will be seen in this patient?","options":["Dendritic ulcer on a fluorescien dye","Satellite lesion","Reverse hypopyon","Ring abscess"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Satellite lesion\\n\\nSatellite lesions are characteristic of fungal ulcer.\\n\\nThe given clinical vignette and the image of an\xa0ulcer\xa0with\xa0irregular feathery margins\xa0are suggestive of a\xa0fungal corneal ulcer. Satellite lesions\xa0are a feature of fungal ulcers.\\n\\nA fungal corneal ulcer is usually caused by trauma with\xa0vegetative\xa0or organic matter.\xa0Signs\xa0are more pronounced than symptoms. The corneal ulcer\xa0appears\xa0dry.\\n\\nCausative organisms:\\n\\n• Aspergillus\\n• Fusarium\\n• Candida albicans\\n\\nSigns of a fungal ulcer:\\n\\n• Irregular margins of the ulcer\\n• Presence of\xa0satellite lesions\xa0surrounding the ulcer.\\n• An immune ring (Wesseley ring) may be seen around the ulcer due to the deposition of the immune complexes.\\n• Slough is usually dry, with feathery borders surrounded by a yellow line of demarcation.\\n• Hypopyon may be present or absent. If present, it is thick, and immobile due to the direct invasion of the fungal hyphae into the anterior chamber.\\n\\nThe image given below shows a fungal ulcer:\\n\\nTreatment:\\n\\nTopical drugs: These include topical natamycin, voriconazole, or amphotericin B drops, which are effective against Aspergillus and Fusarium. Nystatin is effective against Candida.\\nSystemic drugs: Severe ulcers with hypopyon, perforation, or suspected endophthalmitis might warrant the use of oral antifungal agents such as ketoconazole or voriconazole.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/cde71d240a79eec22bd1.png"}]},{"n":16,"subject":"Ophthalmology","text":"Identify the condition below which presents as painless loss of vision.","options":["Branched retinal vein occlusion (BRVO)","Central retinal arterial occlusion (CRAO)","Branched retinal artery occlusion (BRAO)","Central retinal vein occlusion (CRVO)"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Central retinal vein occlusion (CRVO)\\n\\nThe image shows a\xa0splashed tomato appearance\xa0which is characteristic of\xa0central retinal vein occlusion\xa0(CRVO).\\n\\nCRVO refers to sudden or acute occlusion of the central retinal vein. It presents with a sudden loss of vision. On examination, a relative afferent pupillary defect is present.\\n\\nFunduscopy shows engorged and tortuous retinal veins, massive\xa0retinal hemorrhages\xa0with\xa0splashed tomato appearance, and cotton wool spots. The disc is edematous and hyperemic. Changes that occur later include neovascularization at the disc or periphery with pigmentary changes at the macula.\\n\\nComplications:\\n\\n• Rubeosis iridis or neovascularization of the iris\\n• Neovascular glaucoma (NVG) or 100 day glaucoma\\n• Vitreous hemorrhage\\n\\n100-day glaucoma is a type of neovascular glaucoma (NVG) and is so-called because it typically develops\xa03 months after\xa0the episode of CRVO.\\n\\nTreatment usually involves treating the underlying cause and complications.","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/6d3ac367794c488c8f27.png"]},{"n":17,"subject":"Ophthalmology","text":"Silent choroid on FFA is a feature of","options":["Best\'s disease","Stargardt\'s disease","Age related macular degeneration","Cystoid macular edema"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Stargardt\'s disease\\n\\nSilent choroid / Dark choroid on FFA is a feature of Stargardt\'s disease.\\n\\nThe classic feature of \'silent choroid\' or ‘dark choroid’ is due to masking of background choroidal fluorescence by diffuse RPE abnormality.\\n\\nStargardt disease / Fundus flavimaculatus (FFM)\\n\\nIt constitutes the most common type of macular dystrophy. It is characterized by the accumulation of lipofuscin within the RPE.\\n\\nSigns\\n\\n• The macula :\\n\\n• May initially be normal ,later progressing to an oval ‘snail slime’ or ‘beaten-bronze’ appearance and subsequently to geographic atrophy.\\n\\n• Numerous yellow–white round, oval or fish-shaped lesions at the level of the RPE.\\n\\nInvestigation\\n\\n• Visual fields show central loss\\n• FFA - Dark choroid ,the macula shows mixed hyper and hypofluorescence\\n\\nIndocyanine green angiography (ICGA) shows hypofluorescent spots.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/ba53a3ebc4cb6b02180d.png"}]},{"n":18,"subject":"Ophthalmology","text":"A patient suffers head trauma following which he complains of diplopia. His extraocular motility testing in the 9 gazes is as shown in the image. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":["Abducens nerve palsy","Oculomotor nerve palsy","Trochlear nerve palsy","Medial rectus palsy"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Oculomotor nerve palsy\\n\\nThe image is suggestive of\xa0oculomotor nerve palsy\xa0(3rd\xa0nerve palsy).\\n\\nThe central image shows the eyes in primary gaze where the patient\'s left eye has deviated down and out. There is also profound ptosis due to the weakness of the levator muscle.\xa0Due to the involvement of the superior rectus and medial rectus, elevation and adduction will be impaired. Abduction and depression in the primary position (down and out) due to unopposed action of the lateral rectus and superior oblique muscles. All of these findings indicate\xa0left oculomotor nerve palsy.\\n\\nThe signs of left oculomotor nerve palsy are summarized in the image attached below.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nIn\xa0trochlear nerve palsy\xa0(4th\xa0nerve palsy), the\xa0superior oblique\xa0is affected, causing:\\n\\n• The weakness of\xa0depression:\xa0Mainly seen when the eye is adducted (depression during abducted position is a function of the Inferior rectus)\\n• Weakness of\xa0abduction\\n• Failure of\xa0intorsion:\xa0Cause of diplopia.\\n\\nIn\xa0abducens nerve (6th\xa0nerve palsy), the\xa0lateral rectus\xa0is affected. Due to the involvement of only the lateral rectus, abduction will be impaired.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/4d874c3a3e92c7860994.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/452dfe4b84c0b41cedd4.jpg"}]},{"n":19,"subject":"Ophthalmology","text":"A small child with a high risk of amblyopia is planned to undergo surgical correction for his unilateral simple congenital ptosis. He has poor levator function. What is the treatment of choice in this child?","options":["Fasanella-Servat operation","Levator resection","Mullerectomy","Frontalis sling"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Frontalis sling\\n\\nFrontalis sling\xa0procedure is the treatment of choice for\xa0congenital ptosis with poor levator function.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":20,"subject":"Ophthalmology","text":"3 months following cataract surgery, a patient comes with complaints of decreased visual acuity. On examination, posterior capsular opacification is seen. What type of laser can be used to treat this condition?","options":["Argon flouride","Nd-Yag laser","Femto laser","Argon"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Nd-Yag laser\\n\\nThe given history mentions\xa0decreased visual acuity 3 months post-cataract surgery and the presence of\xa0posterior capsular opacification is suggestive of\xa0after-cataract. A posterior\xa0capsulotomy (creating a hole in the posterior capsule) using an Nd: YAG laser is the mainstay of treatment.\\n\\nPosterior capsular opacification, otherwise known as\xa0after cataract/secondary cataract,\xa0is the\xa0most common late complication\xa0of uncomplicated cataract surgery. It occurs due to the proliferation of lens\xa0epithelial cells\xa0that have been left out inside the capsular bag after cataract surgery.\xa0The rate of posterior capsule opacification following extracapsular cataract extraction (ECCE) and phacoemulsification varies with\xa0the age of the patient, the surgeon, the surgical technique, the type of intraocular lens used, and the duration after surgery.\\n\\nSymptoms of after cataract include:\\n\\n• Reduction of visual acuity\\n• Slowly worsening blurring\\n• Glare and occasionally monocular diplopia\\n\\nThey can present in different patterns such as:\\n\\n• Elschnig\'s pearls: present as\xa0vacuolated (pearl-type) grape-like clusters of swollen cells\\n• Soemmering\'s ring: present as a white annular proliferation that is formed at the periphery of the capsular bag.\\n• Fibrosis type: present as fibroblastic metaplasia of the epithelial cells\\n\\nTreatment:\\n\\n• Nd-YAG laser capsulotomy:\xa0The laser used in\xa0posterior capsulotomy\xa0is\xa0Nd: YAG laser\xa0(1064 nm) whose principle is\xa0photodisruption.\xa0The plasma produced at the laser target expands and disrupts the tissues creating an opening.\\n• Surgical treatment: Posterior capsular opacification is managed by creating an opening on the posterior capsule along the visual axis, a procedure called a\xa0posterior capsulotomy.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/d9ce63bdc1992767ba05.jpg"}]},{"n":21,"subject":"Ophthalmology","text":"A 5-year-old female patient presented for her first complete eye examination after a school vision screening found unequal visual acuity. Her parents have not noted any eye problems. Visual acuity in the right eye was 20/20 and the left eye is 20/80. The rest of the examination including Fundus exam was unremarkable, What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":["Brain lesion","Retinoblastoma","Congenital cataract","Amblyopia"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Amblyopia\\n\\nThe most probable diagnosis from the given scenario is Amblyopia.\\n\\nAmblyopia is an uncorrectable decrease in vision in one or both eyes with no apparent structural abnormality seen to explain it. It is a diagnosis of exclusion. Generally, a difference of two lines or more on an eye chart test of visual acuity between the two eyes or a best-corrected vision of 20/30 or worse would be defined as amblyopia. For example, our patient has 20/20 vision with the right eye and only 20/80 with the left and the left eye even after best-corrected vision couldn\'t achieve <20/30 vision, the left eye is said to be amblyopic.\\n\\nClinical features:\\n\\n• Decreased contrast sensitivity\\n• Diminution of vision\\n• Sunglass effect- brightness is less compared to the normal eye\\n• Crowding phenomenon- slipping of images\\n\\nTreatment of choice:\\n\\n• Occlusion of the normal eye (4 to 6 hours/day) using patches.\\n• Penalization:\xa0Blurring of vision using atropine.\\n\\nPrism correction is not the right treatment as the child doesn\'t have strabismus.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Brain lesion does not cause difference in visual acuity in both eyes.\\n\\nOption B: Retinoblastoma can be identified by fundoscopy.\\n\\nOption C: Congenital cataract presents with loss of red reflex immediately after birth.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":22,"subject":"Ophthalmology","text":"Identify the surgical step shown in the image below.","options":["Capsulorrhexis","Intraocular lens implantation","Lens aspiration","Hydrodissection"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Capsulorrhexis\\n\\nCapsulorrhexis is the step shown in the given image.\\n\\nCapsulorrhexis is a method of anterior capsulotomy,\xa0where an opening is made in the anterior capsule of the lens.\xa0It is one of the steps involved in conventional extracapsular cataract extraction (ECCE).\\n\\nContinuous curvilinear capsulorrhexis\xa0(CCC) is considered to be superior to all the pre-existing methods because when done correctly, it does not leave any edges or tears. Trypan blue dye can be used to stain the lens capsule for better visibility.\\n\\nIn ECCE, a portion of the anterior capsule of the lens is removed, allowing extraction of the lens nucleus and cortex. This leaves the remainder of the anterior capsule, the posterior capsule, and the zonular support intact. The intact lens nucleus is then removed from the eye through a limbal incision.\\n\\nAn anterior capsulotomy is followed by hydrodissection\xa0using a balanced salt solution.\xa0This helps to separate the corticonuclear mass from the capsule.\xa0Removal of lens nucleus and aspiration of the cortex\xa0is done using a two-way irrigation and aspiration cannula. An intraocular lens (IOL) is then implanted and the incision is then closed.\\n\\nSome of the IOLs used are shown in the image below.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/630a73aaeb5aab3e4b41.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/93813f7d862db93e3e8c.png"}]},{"n":23,"subject":"Ophthalmology","text":"An infant is brought with complaints of constant watering of eyes with mucopurulent discharge and a non-pulsatile swelling as shown in the image. What is the possible diagnosis?","options":["Anterior encephalocele","Congenital dacryocystitis","Syringoma","Chalazion"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Congenital dacryocystitis\\n\\nThe given presentation of excessive watering of the eyes\xa0and\xa0swelling\xa0over the lacrimal sac in an infant is suggestive of\xa0congenital dacryocystitis.\\n\\nCongenital dacryocystitis is the chronic inflammation of the lacrimal sac due to the accumulation of secretions. This occurs secondary to an obstruction along the course of the nasolacrimal duct.\\n\\nClinical features are epiphora\xa0progressing to mucopurulent discharge,\xa0positive regurgitation test, and swelling over the lacrimal sac area.\\n\\nThe treatment plan is decided according to the age at presentation.\xa0Massaging over the lacrimal sac and topical antibiotics are used as first-line treatment.\\n\\nManagement\\n\\n• Massage over the lacrimal sac area along with topical antibiotics is a conservative management method that can cure symptoms in infants up to 6-9 months\\n• Probing of the nasolacrimal duct with Bowman\'s probe can be attempted if symptoms do not resolve within 6 months.\\n• Dacryocystorhinostomy may be done if the child is brought late or if all other treatment methods fail.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Anterior encephalocele is a pulsatile swelling in the superomedial part of the orbit. It is caused by the herniation of intracranial content through a congenital defect in the skull.\\n\\nOption C: Syringomas are benign proliferations of eccrine sweat glands and are noted as multiple small papules.\\n\\nOption D: Chalazion is a chronic granulomatous,\xa0painless swelling of the Meibomian gland in the eyelid.","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/767380dc88c9075ab2d0.png"]},{"n":24,"subject":"Ophthalmology","text":"A 50-year-old male patient came with complaints of excessive lacrimation. Ocular examination showed an abnormality similar to what is shown below. Which of the following organism is likely to cause this condition?","options":["Chlamydia trachomatis","Staphylococcus aureus","Herpes simplex type 1","Neiserria gonorrhea"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Chlamydia trachomatis\\n\\nThe above image shows Herbert\'s pits. This finding is seen in trachoma which is caused by Chlamydia trachomatis, more specifically the\xa0TRIC\xa0(trachoma inclusion conjunctivitis) serovars A, B, Ba, C.\\n\\nTrachoma is a form of chronic infective conjunctivitis and is one of the leading causes of blindness. It has an ‘active’ inflammatory stage and a ‘cicatricial’ chronic stage.\\n\\nThe active inflammatory stage\xa0is more prevalent in children and presents with nonspecific symptoms of ocular irritation, foreign body sensation, and discharge (often mild symptoms). Examination findings at this stage show:\\n\\n• Conjunctival follicles containung\xa0Leber cells (multinucleated lymphocytes)\\n• corneal\xa0Herbert\'s follicles\xa0(similar to conjunctival follicles)\\n\\nCicatricial chronic stage is seen in middle age. And when the chronic infection with sequelae sets in, the patient complains of pain, lacrimation, photophobia and later blurring, and finally\xa0loss of vision. Conjunctiva shows\xa0concretions\xa0(whitish deposits of inspissated mucus in depressions called glands of Henle). Corneal signs include:\\n\\n• Herbert pits\xa0(pitted scars in the limbal which are remnants of healed of Herbert follicles)\\n• corneal opacity is seen in later stages.\\n\\nThere can be associated trichiasis\xa0( when at least one eyelash rubs the eyeball) and entropion.\\n\\nAt present\xa0WHO grading with\xa0`FISTO’ is in use.\\n\\nTrachoma is treated according to the WHO classification using the\xa0SAFE strategy\xa0(Surgery, Antibiotics, Facial hygiene, and Environmental improvement). Azithromycin is the\xa0antibiotic of choice, and surgery involves the correction of\xa0trichiasis and entropion. In case of significant corneal scarring, penetrating keratoplasty is done.","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/1902fc0cd60aef5e0be4.jpg"]},{"n":25,"subject":"Ophthalmology","text":"A 24-year-old female patient came with complaints of floaters and blurring of vision in both eyes for a few weeks. Fundoscopic examination shows inferior snow banking. What is the probable diagnosis?","options":["Anterior uveitis","Intermediate uveitis","Posterior uveitis","Fuchs\' uveitis"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Intermediate uveitis\\n\\nThe clinical scenario of a young female presenting with floaters, blurring of vision, and fundoscopy showing\xa0snow banking is suggestive of\xa0intermediate uveitis\xa0or\xa0pars planitis\\n\\nIntermediate uveitis is the inflammation involving pars plana ciliaris, peripheral retina, choroid, and vitreous base. It is predominantly seen in young females and most often presents with floaters and bilateral blurring of vision.\\n\\nSnow banking\xa0is the hallmark of intermediate uveitis.\xa0It refers to greyish white fibrovascular plaques over the pars plana, with or without peripheral retinal periphlebitis. This occurs due to the coalescence of snowball opacities.\\n\\nOther Signs of intermediate uveitis:\\n\\n• Snow-ball opacities (Ant’s egg opacities)-\xa0typical exudates present near the ora Serrata in the\xa0inferior quadrant\\n• Few keratic precipitates (KP) due to spillover anterior uveitis\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Anterior uveitis or iridocyclitis is the inflammation of tissue from the iris up to the pars plicata of the ciliary body. It is the most common form of uveitis. They present with:\\n\\n• Circumcorneal conjunctival hyperemia\\n• Aqueous flare\\n• Keratin precipitates (KPs)\\n• Posterior synechiae\\n\\nOption C: Posterior uveitis is the inflammation of the choroid with or without inflammation of the retina. They are classified as:\\n\\n• Choroiditis\\n• Chorioretinitis\\n• Retinochoroditis\\n• Neuroretinitis\\n\\nOption D: Fuchs\' uveitis\xa0or Fuchs heterochromatic iridocyclitis is a chronic non-granulomatous condition. They are usually unilateral. A difference in color between the iris of both eyes termed heterochromia iridis is characteristically seen here. Posterior synechiae are absent. A characteristic stellate-shaped keratic precipitates are located diffusely over the entire corneal epithelium.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/3d051224556e2b838fd9.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/0232c7d84f21304341bf.jpg"}]},{"n":26,"subject":"Ophthalmology","text":"Identify the lesion in the given image.","options":["Interstitial keratitis","Viral keratitis","Fungal keratitis","Bacterial keratitis"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Viral keratitis\\n\\nThe above image shows a\xa0dendritic ulcer\xa0caused by\xa0herpes simplex virus.\\n\\nHerpes simplex\xa0causes\xa0epithelial keratitis\xa0and in severe forms,\xa0dendritic ulcers\xa0develop. Most of the patients present with\xa0redness, photophobia,\xa0watering,\xa0and blurred vision. On examination, visual acuity and\xa0corneal sensation\xa0are\xa0reduced. Follicular conjunctivitis and blepharitis may develop but are usually mild. Clinical reactivation occurs due to multiple stress factors and causes recurrent infections.\\n\\nThe\xa0fluorescein-stained image under slit-lamp examination shows\xa0dendritic ulcers, a\xa0pathognomic\xa0feature of epithelial keratitis. They are mostly treated with\xa0acyclovir\xa03% ointment or\xa0ganciclovir\xa00.15% gel.","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/1221378e8da0c98e9772.jpg"]},{"n":27,"subject":"Ophthalmology","text":"A patient\'s eye was hit by a tennis ball and developed Hyphema. What is the likely source of bleeding ?","options":["Posterior Ciliary artery","Anterior ciliary artery","Circulus arteriosus minor","Circulus arteriosus major"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Circulus arteriosus major\\n\\nCirculus arteriosus major is responsible for Hyphaema (blood in the anterior chamber).\\n\\nHyphaema:\\n\\nCauses of Hyphaema:\\n\\n• Ocular injuries\\n• Postoperative\\n• Herpes zoster iritis\\n• Gonococcal iritis\\n• Intraocular tumor\\n• Spontaneous (from rubeosis iridis)\\n\\nTwo-way irrigation and aspiration cannula can be used for aspirating hyphaema.\\n\\nBlood Supply of Eye:\\n\\nCornea:\xa0Avascular\\n\\nUvea:\xa03 arteries are important.\\n\\n• Short post ciliary artery- Choroid\\n• Long post ciliary artery-2 in number join each other near ciliary muscle with anterior ciliary arteries to form circulus arteriolar major (C A major).\\n• Short anterior ciliary arteries join long posterior ciliary arteries to form C A major.\\n• Branches from circulus arteriolar major go to the pupillary border to form circulus arteriosus minor(C A minor).\\n\\nRetina:\\n\\n• Outer four layers-choriocapillaries\xa0(RPE, rods and cones, ELM, outer nuclear layer)\\n• Inner layers- Central retinal artery\\n• Macula-\xa0Branches of central retinal artery + Choriocapillaries.\\n• Foveola- Avascular\\n\\nOptic Nerve Head:\\n\\n• Surface nerve fibre layer- From retinal arteriole.\\n• Lamina cribrosa- From ciliary vessels derived from posterior ciliary artery and arterial circle of Zinn and Haller.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/261577a547baa9e91f1a.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/1ab49fc9139978d03be2.jpg"}]},{"n":28,"subject":"Ophthalmology","text":"A 65-year-old woman presented with sudden, progressive, painless loss of vision in the right eye. There was no history of associated photopsia. On examination, shifting fluid sign is present. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":["Rhegmatogenous RD","Retinal dialysis","Exudative RD","Traction RD"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Exudative RD\\n\\nThe given clinical scenario with\xa0the\xa0presence of shifting fluid\xa0is suggestive of\xa0exudative retinal detachment.\\n\\nExudative RD\xa0is characterized by the accumulation of fluid beneath the retina in the absence of a retinal break or traction. Fluid leaks outside the vessels and accumulates under the retina. RD does not occur as long as the retinal pigment epithelium is pumping the leaking fluid into the choroidal circulation.\xa0Causes:\\n\\n• Inflammatory conditions like choroiditis, posterior scleritis.\\n• Choroidal tumours like melanoma.\\n• Toxaemia of pregnancy\\n• Central serous retinopathy\\n• Coats disease\\n\\nPatients may present with painless progressive visual field defects without photopsia. Floaters may be present if there is an associated vitritis. Clinical signs include:\\n\\n• Convex configuration of the retinal detachment, with a smooth surface\\n• A detached retina is very mobile\\n• Shifting fluid sign- Subretinal fluid detaches the area of the retina under which it accumulates. In the upright position, it collects under the inferior retina. But in the supine position, the inferior retina flattens, the fluid shifts posteriorly which detach the superior retina.\\n• The cause of the RD may be apparent.\\n• Leopard spots- Scattered areas of clumped subretinal pigment may be seen after the detachment has flattened\\n\\nRetinal detachment (RD) is the result of the accumulation of fluid between the retina and the retinal pigment epithelium. It\xa0is classified into the following:\\n\\n• Rhegmatogenous RD- due to a tear or break in the retina\\n• Secondary RD - due to some other preceding event\\n\\n• Exudative RD\\n• Tractional RD\\n\\nThe image shows the shifting fluid sign and late-phase\xa0fluorescein angiogram showing hyperfluorescent spots with a\xa0leopard spot pattern\xa0in the posterior fundus and staining of the arteries and an avascular area in the nasal region respectively.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/021dacd5309a10dc6d4e.jpg"}]},{"n":29,"subject":"Ophthalmology","text":"What is the use of this surgical instrument?","options":["Dilate the punctum of lacrimal gland","Curette the lacrimal sac in cases of epiphora","Probing the nasolacrimal duct","Syringing the lacrimal passage"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Probing the nasolacrimal duct\\n\\nThe given image shows\xa0Bowman\'s lacrimal probe.\xa0 It is identified\xa0by the ends of the lacrimal probe which are blunt with a central stable platform. It is used to probe the nasolacrimal duct in the management of\xa0congenital nasolacrimal duct obstruction.\xa0It is also used to identify the lacrimal sac during dacryocystorhinostomy and dacryocystectomy.\\n\\nOther common instruments used in lacrimal gland surgeries:\\n\\nNettleship lacrimal punctum dilator (shown in the image attached below) is used to dilate the punctum before lacrimal sac surgeries (Option-A)\\n\\nLacrimal gland cannula\xa0is used for syringing the lacrimal gland and the passage.\xa0It is a long curved hypodermic needle with a blunt tip. (Option-C)\\n\\nLacrimal sac curette\xa0is used to curette the lacrimal sac in cases of epiphora (Option-D).\xa0It is a cylindrical instrument, one end of which is a blunt-tipped dissector, and the other end is curetted.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/c60a7a8bf874a3085526.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/7ac2119560895dde233a.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/994568705a1108d8f664.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/9c0378117989d257e4ac.jpg"}]},{"n":30,"subject":"Ophthalmology","text":"A 45-year-old man presents to the casualty with a sudden loss of vision and painful ocular movements. On examination of the affected eye, a relative afferent pupillary defect with a normal optic disc is seen. Visual field testing was done which revealed central scotoma in the same eye. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":["Optic nerve glioma","Neuroretinitis","Eale’s disease","Optic neuritis"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Optic neuritis\\n\\nThe given clinical scenario of a sudden painful loss of vision in the absence of fundoscopic changes, RAPD and central scotoma is suggestive of\xa0optic neuritis,\xa0specifically retrobulbar neuritis.\\n\\nThere are 3 types of optic neuritis:\\n\\n• papillitis\\n• neuroretinitis\\n• retrobulbar neuritis\\n\\nClinically, optic neuritis is characterized by sudden, progressive visual loss.\xa0 Ocular movements are painful in retrobulbar neuritis and painless in papillitis and neuroretinitis. Glowing sensations known as phosphenes may be induced with sound or movement. Color vision may also be impaired.\\n\\nSigns of optic neuritis:\\n\\n• Marked reduction in visual acuity\\n• Impaired color vision\\n• Marcus Gunn Pupil\xa0indicates the relative afferent pupillary defect\xa0(RAPD), is a diagnostic sign. Can be detected by swinging flashlight test\\n• Disc hyperemia with blurred margin\\n• Central or centrocaecal scotoma in the visual field (most common field defect in optic neuritis)\\n\\nType\\nRetrobulbar neuritis\\nPapillitis\\nNeuroretinitis\\n\\nPathology\\nInflammation of the optic nerve behind the disc\\nInflammation of the optic nerve head\\nInflammation of the optic nerve head and the macula\\n\\nFundus appearance\\nOptic disc appears normal\\n\\nHyperemia and oedema of the optic disc with or without\xa0peri-papillary flame-shaped hemorrhages\\n\\nMacular star (due to exudates that form around macula)\\n\\nPrevalence\\nMost common in adults\\n\\nMost common in children\\n\\nLeast common type\\n\\nAssociated conditions\\nStrong association with demyelinating disorders like\xa0multiple sclerosis\xa0(MS) and neuromyelitis optica (NMO or Devic\'s disease).\\n\\nWeak association with demyelinating disorders\\n\\nCat-scratch disease, Lyme disease, Syphilis etc.\\n\\nThe image below shows papillitis.\\n\\nThe image below shows the\xa0macular star appearance in neuroretinitis.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/7080d823c61f8c22be22.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/cda9a78a673934e0f175.png"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/3e5000e1bf6956677bdf.png"}]}]}')}}]);